 digits in the ten-thousands place, including the carry-over digit:.\n\n```.\n  485989.\n+ 455311.\n---------.\n100000.\n```.\n\nSince 8 + 5 + 1 = 14, we write down 4 and carry over 1 to the next column (the hundred-thousands place).\n\n```.\n  485989.\n+ 455311.\n---------.\n100000.\n```.\n\nNext, we add the digits in the hundred-thousands place, including the carry-over digit:.\n\n```.\n  485989.\n+ 455311.\n---------.\n1000000.\n```.\n\nSince 4 + 4 + 1 = 9, we write down 9.\n\n```.\n  485989.\n+ 455311.\n---------.\n1000000.\n```.\n\nFinally, we add the digits in the millions place, which are both 0:.\n\n```.\n  485989.\n+ 455311.\n---------.\n1000000.\n```.\n\nSince 0 + 0 = 0, we write down 0.\n\n```.\n  485989.\n+ 455311.\n---------.\n1000000.\n```.\n\nTherefore, the sum of 485989 and 455311 is 1000000.\n</think>.\n<answer>. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
